WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.697
File system analysis helps analysts understand what changed on disk and when.

00:00:05.697 --> 00:00:10.550
File metadata can include ownership, permissions, size, paths, and timestamps.

00:00:10.550 --> 00:00:16.669
Linux files commonly have modified, accessed, and changed times, often called MAC times.

00:00:16.669 --> 00:00:19.834
Modified time usually reflects content changes.

00:00:19.834 --> 00:00:25.531
Accessed time may reflect reads, though mount options can reduce its reliability.

00:00:25.531 --> 00:00:30.384
Changed time reflects metadata changes such as permissions or ownership.

00:00:30.384 --> 00:00:36.925
Permissions and ownership can reveal whether files are accessible to unexpected users or groups.

00:00:36.925 --> 00:00:47.686
Hidden files, dot directories, recently modified files, unusual executable permissions, files in temporary locations, and files outside package-managed paths can all be worth review.

00:00:47.686 --> 00:00:51.273
None of these signals is conclusive alone.

00:00:51.273 --> 00:00:54.438
They are leads that need context.

00:00:54.438 --> 00:01:00.135
Deleted file considerations depend on collection method, filesystem, system activity, and timing.

00:01:00.135 --> 00:01:06.676
Sometimes full disk images or snapshots preserve more context than a live targeted collection.

00:01:06.676 --> 00:01:13.217
In other cases, operational urgency or cloud architecture may limit what can be collected.

00:01:13.217 --> 00:01:20.180
The analyst should document the collection method and explain how it affects confidence and limitations.

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:26.299
Timelines become powerful when filesystem metadata is combined with logs and other evidence.

00:01:26.299 --> 00:01:37.904
A service file change, followed by an authentication event, followed by a process start, followed by network connections, tells a stronger story than any single event.

00:01:37.904 --> 00:01:46.133
Good timelines separate observed facts from interpretation and identify gaps, uncertainty, time zone issues, and possible clock differences.
